\subsection{Quantum Noise Limit and Callen-Welton Formalism}
At millikelvin scales ($T \le 20\text{ mK}$), classical Nyquist formulas fail as the thermal energy $k_B T$ falls below the zero-point energy level ($h f$). The full Callen-Welton quantum-corrected single-sided noise power spectral density (PSD) per unit bandwidth is given by:
\begin{equation}
S_{V}(f) = 4 R \left[ \frac{hf}{\exp\left(\frac{hf}{k_B T}\right) - 1} + \frac{hf}{2} \right]
\end{equation}
At $f = 6.0\text{ GHz}$ and $T = 20\text{ mK}$, $hf/k_B T \approx 14.4 \gg 1$, driving the channel into the quantum-limited regime where vacuum fluctuations dominate ($S_V \approx 2 R h f$).

